{
  "body_human_readable": "There are several tools available for working with ontologies, known as Ontology Development Environments (ODEs). Some of the most popular and widely used tools include:\n\n1. Prot\u00e9g\u00e9: An open-source ontology editor and knowledge store developed by the Stanford Center for Biomedical Informatics Research. It is the most complete and popular option for editing ontologies, supporting OWL, RDF/XML, and OWL/XML languages. Prot\u00e9g\u00e9 offers modules for creating, editing, and visualizing ontologies, and supports integration with reasoning engines and plugins for extended functionality. It is widely used in the biomedical domain and beyond, with a large user community and comprehensive documentation [1] [2] [3].\n\n2. TopBraid Composer: A visual, all-encompassing knowledge modeling environment for building and maintaining domain models and ontologies in the Semantic Web. It supports various standards and offers features for integration, visualization, and collaboration. However, the free version is no longer available [3].\n\n3. OntoStudio: A multilingual editor and ontology modeler with customization options, supporting OWL, RDF, and ObjectLogic formats. It allows collaborative editing, supports various storage types, and includes a query builder for database integration [4].\n\n4. Fluent Editor: A free and open-source tool that allows users to edit ontologies in natural language, with support for the majority of Semantic Web W3C standards. It offers features like interactive diagrams, SWRL debugging, and integration with R for statistical analysis [5].\n\n5. VocBench: An open-source ODE used by many businesses, supporting OWL ontologies, SKOS, and other RDF datasets. It is designed for collaborative editing and management of ontologies [3].\n\n6. Swoop: A web-oriented ontology editor, fully implemented and accessible for OWL. It provides browser-based features for editing, browsing, and managing ontologies, with a focus on usability and integration [3].\n\n7. OBO-Edit: A particular ontology editor for biologists, supporting the creation and maintenance of biological ontologies [3].\n\nThese tools vary in terms of usability, scalability, stability, integration, documentation, and originality. For example, Prot\u00e9g\u00e9 is noted for its stability, integration capabilities, and comprehensive documentation, while tools like Fluent Editor and VocBench offer unique features for specific use cases [6].\n\nIn summary, the main tools for working with ontologies are Prot\u00e9g\u00e9, TopBraid Composer, OntoStudio, Fluent Editor, VocBench, Swoop, and OBO-Edit, each with its own strengths and areas of application [7] [6] [3].",
